-- Q3759: SOC Engagements [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SOC 1 Type II examining operating effectiveness","SOC 2 Type II examining operating effectiveness","SOC Type I evaluating control design suitability","SOC 3 general-use report with seal of assurance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320.08, a Type I engagement evaluates the suitability of control design at a specific point in time. This is appropriate for organizations with recently implemented controls that lack sufficient operating history for a Type II.
Wrong (A): A SOC 1 Type II requires controls to have operated effectively over a period (typically six months or more), which a newly implemented environment cannot demonstrate.
Wrong (B): A SOC 2 Type II similarly requires an extended operating period to test operating effectiveness, not just design suitability.
Wrong (D): A SOC 3 is a general-use report that provides a seal of assurance but does not provide the detailed control design evaluation needed here.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3759;

-- Q3762: SOC Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An entity that uses the service organization''s services and whose financial reporting may be affected","The CPA firm that performs the SOC examination on the service organization","The regulatory body that oversees and monitors the service organization","The standards body that develops and publishes the Trust Services Criteria"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.08), a user entity is defined as an entity that uses the services of the service organization, where its internal control over financial reporting may be affected. User entities and their auditors are the primary intended users of SOC 1 reports.
Wrong (B): The entity performing the SOC examination is the service auditor, not the user entity. The service auditor provides an independent opinion on controls.
Wrong (C): Regulatory bodies may oversee certain industries but are not defined as user entities under the SSAE 18 framework.
Wrong (D): The AICPA develops the Trust Services Criteria but is not a user entity in the context of SOC engagements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3762;

-- Q3763: SOC Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service organization''s complete financial statements and tax returns","The user entity''s internal control policies and procedures manual","The services provided, system components, and boundaries of the system","The service auditor''s detailed testing methodology and sampling approach"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per SSAE No. 18 (AT-C Section 320.35), the system description must include the types of services provided, system components (infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data), and the boundaries of the system. This enables user entities to understand the scope of controls examined.
Wrong (A): Financial statements are not part of the SOC system description. The system description focuses on the service delivery environment, not the organization''s financial reporting.
Wrong (B): User entity control policies are the responsibility of the user entity, not the service organization. CUECs may be listed but are not the user entity''s own policies.
Wrong (D): The service auditor''s testing methodology is part of the auditor''s workpapers and the tests-and-results section, not the system description prepared by management.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3763;

-- Q3766: SOC Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Detailed testing results for the subservice organization''s operating controls","A separate auditor opinion on the subservice organization''s control environment","The subservice organization''s independently audited financial statements","The subservice organization''s identity, services provided, and expected controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per SSAE No. 18 (AT-C Section 320.14), when the carve-out method is used, the system description must identify the subservice organization, describe the nature of services provided, and disclose the types of controls expected to be in place. The service auditor does not test those controls under this method.
Wrong (A): Under the carve-out method, the service auditor does not test the subservice organization''s controls, so detailed testing results would not be available or required.
Wrong (B): A separate opinion on the subservice organization is not required; the carve-out method explicitly excludes those controls from the engagement scope.
Wrong (C): The subservice organization''s financial statements are not relevant to the SOC system description, which focuses on control environment details.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3766;

-- Q3767: SOC Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The description of tests of controls performed and the results of those tests","The service organization''s marketing materials included as report appendices","The service auditor''s original engagement letter and fee schedule","The service organization''s employee handbook and HR policy documents"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C Section 320, the tests-and-results section details the specific controls tested, procedures applied, and any exceptions identified. This is the most critical section for user entity auditors assessing the reliability of controls affecting their clients'' financial reporting.
Wrong (B): Marketing materials are not part of a SOC 1 report. The report contains the system description, auditor''s opinion, and tests-and-results section.
Wrong (C): The engagement letter governs the terms between the service auditor and service organization and is not included in the SOC 1 report distributed to user entities.
Wrong (D): Employee handbooks are internal HR documents and do not form part of the SOC 1 report structure.$EXPL$
