-- Q3780: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["ISO 27001 information security management standard","NIST Cybersecurity Framework for critical infrastructure","COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework (2013)","COBIT 2019 governance and management framework"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSC 2017), the common criteria CC1 through CC9 are deliberately mapped to the 17 principles of the COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework (2013). This alignment ensures the Trust Services framework builds on the widely accepted internal control foundation.
Wrong (A): ISO 27001 is an information security management standard focused on establishing an ISMS. While complementary, the TSC common criteria were not mapped to ISO 27001.
Wrong (B): The NIST Cybersecurity Framework addresses IT-specific risk management but is not the framework to which the TSC common criteria are formally aligned.
Wrong (D): COBIT 2019 provides IT governance and management guidance but is not the direct mapping target for the TSC common criteria structure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3780;

-- Q3781: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["How the entity communicates internally about its control responsibilities","How the entity identifies and assesses risks that could affect the system","How the entity selects, develops, and performs ongoing monitoring activities","How the entity restricts access, manages credentials, and removes access rights"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (2017), CC6 (Logical and Physical Access Controls) addresses how the entity restricts logical and physical access, manages user credentials, prevents unauthorized access, and removes access rights when no longer appropriate. CC6 encompasses provisioning, authentication, and access reviews.
Wrong (A): Internal communication about control responsibilities falls under CC2 (Communication and Information), not CC6.
Wrong (B): Identifying and assessing risks is addressed by CC3 (Risk Assessment), which focuses on evaluating threats to objectives.
Wrong (C): Selecting and developing monitoring activities falls under CC4 (Monitoring Activities), which governs ongoing evaluation of internal controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3781;

-- Q3785: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Controls that the service organization implements as backup safeguard measures","Controls tested by the service auditor at each user entity''s location","Controls that a carved-out subservice organization must separately implement","Controls user entities must implement to achieve the related control objectives"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.A33), complementary user entity controls (CUECs) are controls that the service organization assumes are in place at user entities. These complement the service organization''s controls and are necessary for overall criteria achievement, such as user entities managing their own access provisioning.
Wrong (A): Backup measures implemented by the service organization are part of its own control environment, not CUECs. CUECs are implemented by the user entities themselves.
Wrong (B): The service auditor does not test controls at user entity locations. CUECs are the user entity''s responsibility to implement and evaluate.
Wrong (C): Controls at subservice organizations are called complementary subservice organization controls (CSOCs), not CUECs. CSOCs apply under the carve-out method.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3785;

-- Q3787: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The NIST Privacy Framework as the sole authoritative source","The GDPR as the exclusive international privacy standard applied","The HIPAA Privacy Rule as the sole healthcare privacy standard","Generally Accepted Privacy Principles (GAPP) and applicable regulations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (2017), the privacy category is aligned with the AICPA''s Generally Accepted Privacy Principles (GAPP). These criteria address notice, choice and consent, collection, use, retention, access, disclosure, quality, and monitoring throughout the data lifecycle, incorporating applicable privacy regulations.
Wrong (A): The NIST Privacy Framework is a separate voluntary framework. While useful, it is not the basis for the TSC privacy criteria.
Wrong (B): The GDPR applies to EU data subjects but is not the exclusive framework underlying the TSC privacy category. GAPP is broader in scope.
Wrong (C): HIPAA applies specifically to protected health information. The TSC privacy criteria are based on GAPP, which covers personal information more broadly across industries.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3787;

