-- Q3791: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the exceptions since they are solely the service organization''s responsibility","Evaluate whether exceptions affect the user entity''s risk assessment and controls","Immediately terminate the contract with the cloud service provider","File a formal complaint with the AICPA about the service organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C Section 205 and AICPA SOC guidance, when a SOC 2 report includes CC6 exceptions, user entity management should evaluate the nature and significance of the exceptions against their own risk profile. This includes assessing whether complementary user entity controls (CUECs) mitigate the identified risks.
Wrong (A): Ignoring exceptions is inappropriate. User entities have a responsibility to assess how service organization control deficiencies affect their own risk environment.
Wrong (C): Immediately terminating a contract is a disproportionate response. Exceptions should be evaluated for significance before making vendor relationship decisions.
Wrong (D): Filing an AICPA complaint is not the appropriate response to control exceptions. The user entity should focus on its own risk assessment and compensating controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3791;

-- Q3793: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Encrypt all internal communications using end-to-end encryption protocols","Obtain and communicate relevant quality information to support internal control","Publish all system information on its publicly accessible website","Restrict all communications to formal written channels and memoranda"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria CC2 (Communication and Information), an entity must obtain and generate relevant, quality information and communicate it internally and externally to support the functioning of internal control. This includes communicating control responsibilities and establishing channels for reporting issues.
Wrong (A): End-to-end encryption is a security control under CC6 (Logical and Physical Access Controls), not a CC2 requirement. CC2 addresses information flow, not encryption methods.
Wrong (C): Publishing all system information publicly would violate confidentiality principles. CC2 requires appropriate communication channels, not unrestricted public disclosure.
Wrong (D): Restricting communication to formal written channels only would impede effective information flow. CC2 requires communication through appropriate channels, not exclusively formal written ones.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3793;

-- Q3794: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity identifies and assesses risks to its objectives, including fraud risks","The entity quantifies every identified risk in precise monetary dollar terms","The entity eliminates all identified risks before issuance of the SOC report","The entity delegates all risk assessment activities to external consultants"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria CC3 (Risk Assessment), an entity must specify clear objectives, identify and analyze risks to achieving those objectives — including fraud risks — and assess potential environmental changes affecting internal control. Risk assessment is ongoing and informs control design.
Wrong (B): CC3 does not require risks to be quantified in monetary terms. Risk assessment involves identifying and analyzing risks but does not mandate precise dollar-amount quantification.
Wrong (C): Eliminating all risks is neither possible nor required. CC3 requires identification and assessment of risks so that appropriate mitigation controls can be designed and implemented.
Wrong (D): Management retains responsibility for risk assessment. While consultants may assist, delegating the entire process to external parties does not satisfy CC3 requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3794;

