-- Q3804: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The exceptions should be omitted from the report since they are isolated incidents","The exceptions must be described in the tests-and-results section with impact evaluation","The auditor must issue an adverse opinion whenever any exceptions are identified","The exceptions should only be communicated verbally to the service organization management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18, all exceptions identified during testing must be described in the tests-and-results section regardless of whether they are isolated or systemic. The service auditor evaluates the nature, cause, frequency, and significance of exceptions to determine their impact on the overall opinion.
Wrong (A): Omitting exceptions from the report would violate reporting standards. All identified exceptions must be disclosed in the tests-and-results section, even if isolated.
Wrong (C): Isolated exceptions do not automatically require an adverse opinion. The auditor assesses whether exceptions are material enough to affect the overall conclusion on controls.
Wrong (D): Verbal-only communication of exceptions is insufficient. Professional standards require written disclosure in the report to ensure user entities are informed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3804;

-- Q3805: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s financial obligations to its lenders and debt covenants","The entity''s commitments to employees through employment contracts","Commitments made to user entities through service agreements and policies","The entity''s regulatory compliance commitments to government agencies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AICPA Trust Services Criteria CC2.3, principal service commitments are declarations made to user entities about how the system will perform and be managed, typically found in service level agreements, contracts, and published policies. System requirements are the specifications needed to fulfill those commitments.
Wrong (A): Financial obligations to lenders are debt-related matters, not principal service commitments within the Trust Services framework. These relate to the entity''s capital structure, not its service delivery.
Wrong (B): Employment commitments are internal HR obligations to the workforce. Principal service commitments specifically address promises made to user entities about service delivery.
Wrong (D): While regulatory compliance may be relevant, principal service commitments specifically refer to commitments made to user entities, not to government or regulatory agencies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3805;

-- Q3809: IT General Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Removing all password requirements to reduce user authentication burden","Requiring password changes every 30 days for more frequent credential rotation","Allowing single-character passwords to improve ease of user access","Monitoring for compromised credentials and requiring changes only when detected"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-63B (Section 5.1.1.2) recommends against mandatory periodic password expiration because it drives users toward predictable password variations. Instead, organizations should monitor for compromised credentials through breach databases and threat intelligence, requiring changes only upon evidence of compromise.
Wrong (A): Removing all password requirements would eliminate a fundamental authentication control and violate NIST guidelines requiring memorized secret verifiers.
Wrong (B): More frequent mandatory rotation (every 30 days) worsens the problem by further incentivizing weak, predictable password patterns.
Wrong (C): Single-character passwords would provide virtually no security. NIST SP 800-63B requires a minimum password length of at least eight characters.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3809;

-- Q3810: IT General Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It reduces credentials users manage, decreasing weak or reused password risk","It eliminates the need for any authentication controls across applications","It automatically encrypts all data transmitted between connected applications","It removes the need for implementing multi-factor authentication controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-63C (Federation and Assertions), SSO centralizes authentication to a single identity provider, reducing credential fatigue. By authenticating once, users avoid managing multiple passwords, which decreases the likelihood of weak passwords and credential reuse across applications.
Wrong (B): SSO does not eliminate the need for authentication controls. It centralizes authentication but still requires robust controls at the identity provider, including strong password policies.
Wrong (C): SSO handles authentication, not data encryption. Transport-layer encryption (TLS) is a separate control that protects data in transit between applications.
Wrong (D): SSO should be paired with MFA to prevent a single compromised credential from granting access to all connected applications. SSO increases the need for MFA.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3810;

-- Q3813: IT General Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It automatically detects and fixes bugs found in the source code","It prevents all unauthorized access to the application source code","It eliminates the need for any software testing before deployment","It maintains a complete audit trail of changes and enables version rollback"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06.01, version control systems maintain a complete history of all code changes including who made each change and when, providing an audit trail. This enables rollback to previous versions if issues are discovered and supports compliance verification.
Wrong (A): Version control tracks changes but does not automatically detect or fix bugs. Bug detection requires separate testing and code review processes.
Wrong (B): While version control can include access controls, it does not prevent all unauthorized access. Access management requires separate authentication and authorization controls.
Wrong (C): Version control complements but does not replace software testing. Changes tracked in version control still require unit, integration, and acceptance testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3813;

-- Q3814: IT General Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ensuring correct job sequencing, monitoring for failures, and handling exceptions","Allowing any employee to submit and modify batch jobs without restriction","Running all batch jobs manually to maintain direct personal oversight","Scheduling all batch jobs simultaneously to maximize system utilization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under COBIT 2019 DSS01.01 (Perform Operational Procedures), batch job scheduling controls must ensure critical processing jobs execute in the correct sequence, complete successfully, and produce expected results. Monitoring for failures with documented exception-handling procedures prevents processing gaps affecting financial reporting integrity.
Wrong (B): Unrestricted batch job submission creates a significant control weakness. Proper access controls and authorization are required to prevent unauthorized or erroneous job modifications.
Wrong (C): Manual execution increases the risk of human error and is not scalable. Automated scheduling with proper monitoring is more reliable than manual oversight for recurring jobs.
Wrong (D): Running all jobs simultaneously ignores sequencing dependencies. Jobs that depend on prior outputs must execute in the correct order to produce accurate results.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3814;

-- Q3819: IT General Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Requirements gathering and business analysis","System design and architecture planning","Testing and quality assurance verification","Production deployment and release management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT 2019 BAI07 (Manage IT Change Acceptance and Transitioning), the testing and quality assurance phase verifies that the developed solution meets specified requirements through unit, integration, system, and user acceptance testing. This phase identifies defects before production deployment.
Wrong (A): Requirements gathering defines what the solution should accomplish but does not verify whether the implemented solution meets those specifications.
Wrong (B): The design phase establishes the technical architecture and approach but does not validate that the built solution functions correctly against requirements.
Wrong (D): Deployment moves the tested solution into production. Verification of requirements conformance should occur during testing, before the deployment phase begins.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3819;

-- Q3824: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Backup and recovery management controls","Capacity management and performance planning","Physical security and environmental controls","Privileged access management and segregation of duties"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, AC-5 (Separation of Duties) and AC-6 (Least Privilege), privileged access management controls must prevent administrators from unilaterally granting themselves elevated access without independent approval. Self-granting privileges violates segregation of duties, a fundamental IT general control.
Wrong (A): Backup and recovery controls address data protection and disaster recovery procedures, not access management or privilege escalation issues.
Wrong (B): Capacity management deals with system performance and resource planning, which is unrelated to unauthorized privilege escalation by administrators.
Wrong (C): Physical security addresses facility access and environmental protections. This scenario involves logical access controls and privilege management, not physical entry.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3824;

-- Q3825: IT General Controls [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To provide developers with a playground for experimenting with new technologies","To identify potential issues and confirm changes work before impacting production","To increase the organization''s total server count for audit documentation","To keep the QA team occupied with testing between scheduled production releases"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06.01, changes must be tested in an environment mirroring production before deployment. A staging environment identifies compatibility issues, performance problems, and functional defects under production-like conditions before changes reach live systems.
Wrong (A): While staging environments allow testing, their primary purpose is risk reduction, not experimentation. Developer experimentation typically occurs in separate sandbox environments.
Wrong (C): Server count is irrelevant to audit requirements. The staging environment exists to validate changes, not to demonstrate infrastructure capacity to auditors.
Wrong (D): The staging environment serves a critical control function in change management. Its purpose is risk mitigation, not workload distribution for the QA team.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3825;

-- Q3826: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A change management risk related to unauthorized system modifications","A segregation of duties conflict that could enable fraudulent payments","A physical security vulnerability exposing sensitive facility areas","A backup and recovery deficiency affecting data restoration capability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA Trust Services Criteria CC6.1 and COBIT 2019 DSS06.03, having both payroll processing and vendor account approval capabilities creates a segregation of duties conflict. The clerk could create a fictitious vendor, approve the account, and route fraudulent payments without requiring collusion.
Wrong (A): Change management risks involve unauthorized modifications to systems or applications. This scenario involves incompatible access roles in business processes, not system changes.
Wrong (C): Physical security vulnerabilities relate to facility access and environmental controls. This is a logical access control issue involving incompatible role combinations.
Wrong (D): Backup and recovery deficiencies affect data protection and restoration. The payroll clerk''s dual access represents an authorization control weakness, not a data recovery issue.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3826;
